data: {"type":"block","block":{"type":"WritingDraft","id":"turn-6c7ad99a2de4-writing-draft","meta":{"status":"ready","error":null},"data":{"kind":"abstract","title":"Retrieval in Retrieval-Augmented Generation: Sources, Strategies and Evaluation","sections":[{"heading":"Abstract","segments":[{"text":"The sources treat retrieval as the step in RAG that efficiently finds relevant documents in a data source, and they point to the retrieval source, granularity, pre-processing and embedding model as its key issues.","citationIds":["c1"],"speaker":null},{"text":"Retrieval sources have broadened from plain text to semi-structured and structured data such as knowledge graphs, and recent work increasingly retrieves content generated by LLMs themselves.","citationIds":["c1"],"speaker":null},{"text":"Beyond single-pass retrieval, the survey describes iterative retrieval, which repeatedly searches the knowledge base using the query and the text generated so far, and notes that this improves the robustness of later answers.","citationIds":["c2"],"speaker":null},{"text":"Adaptive approaches such as FLARE and Self-RAG go further by judging whether retrieval is needed in a given scenario rather than following a fixed retrieval process.","citationIds":["c3"],"speaker":null},{"text":"On the evaluation side, one study judges a document retrieved if any of its chunks is retrieved, and stresses balancing recall with precision and F1 because the number of retrieved chunks is fixed while the number of retrieved documents varies.","citationIds":["c4"],"speaker":null}]}],"citations":[{"id":"c1","chunkId":"396a0fadeb4cd40f_c_f22af6d01a52a8fb","docId":"396a0fadeb4cd40f","docTitle":"2312.10997_Retrieval_Augmented_Generation_for_Large_Language_Models_A_Survey","page":5,"quote":"it is crucial to efficiently retrieve relevant documents from the data source"},{"id":"c2","chunkId":"396a0fadeb4cd40f_c_18d9f568d1c19f4e","docId":"396a0fadeb4cd40f","docTitle":"2312.10997_Retrieval_Augmented_Generation_